{{- /*
  ~/.pi/agent/subagents.json — pi-subagents runtime policy.

  agentTiers is the catalogue of named model profiles. Agent files do not pin a
  profile; the host may pass a catalogue key on an Agent call for explicit routing.
  If omitted, agentTiers.defaultTier applies (medium). The host cannot pass model
  or thinking directly.

  The ordinary Agent catalogue is the single model-policy source. It keeps its
  user-facing `low | medium | high` keys and is machine-scoped:
    work machine:    low = luna/xhigh, medium = luna/max,   high = astra/high
    private machine: low = luna/max,   medium = astra/high, high = astra/xhigh

  work is private shifted down exactly one rung. That is the design: the two
  machines share one ladder — luna/xhigh, luna/max, astra/high, astra/xhigh —
  and differ only in where they enter it. Read a rung's cost once and it means
  the same thing on both. astra appearing on work at all is new as of
  2026-09-06; work was luna-only through the whole sol era.

  The parent session is the medium/default baseline and is emitted separately by
  modify_settings.json.tmpl: work -> luna/max, private -> astra/high. That is
  this catalogue's `medium` row verbatim, and it is meant to stay that way, so
  that an interactive turn and a spawn that names no tier cost the same. Keep
  the two in lockstep whenever either moves, and do not collapse the rungs back
  to one model/effort without an explicit policy change.

  Protocol v4 (pi-subagents >= 1.9.0 with pi-workflows >= 1.5.0) removed the
  separate `small | medium | large` workflow vocabulary and the layer that mapped
  it: a managed workflow spawn names a key from THIS catalogue and
  resolveAgentTier() is the only resolver. Per-call `model`/`thinking` are now
  rejected by the validator, and the retired `workflow` settings key must not be
  reintroduced — `agentTiers.defaultTier` below replaces `workflow.defaultTier`.
  The two packages move together: pi-workflows declares `pi-subagents >= 1.9.0`
  and fails its startup handshake against an older peer.

  pi-workflows >= 1.6.0 has a `strengths` setting of its own, mapping a workflow
  strength onto a key from THIS catalogue. It is not a reintroduction of that
  layer and must not become one: what v4 deleted carried its own model and
  thinking values, so a workflow spawn was resolved by a second resolver against
  a second policy, whereas `strengths` carries only keys from here.
  resolveAgentTier() stays the only resolver and this catalogue the only model
  policy. Nothing about it belongs in this file — the vocabulary is pi-workflows'
  business, the models are this machine's — and no `workflow` key may reappear
  here. This machine defines no strengths, so each one runs on the profile of the
  same name below and the built-in workflows resolve `low` and `medium` exactly
  as any other spawn does. That also means workflow fan-outs share these rungs
  with every ordinary spawn: pricing workflow work separately would mean adding
  a profile here and pointing a strength at it from pi-workflows, not editing a
  rung the rest of the catalogue uses.

  One shift in 1.6.0 to be aware of when reading costs off this file: the
  `verify` and `judgePanel` quality helpers now request `low` where they used to
  name no tier and fall through to `medium`. `completenessCheck` requests
  `medium`, which is where it already landed. A tier a call requests outranks
  defaultTier, so those three no longer follow it.

  low/medium/high are also the profile names pi-subagents ships (all inheriting
  the parent model), so the built-in workflows and the ad-hoc script resolve
  against real profiles here instead of being dropped to the default. The shipped
  `fast` profile is gone; nothing here or in dot_pi/agent/agents/ may name it. A
  managed call that names no tier falls back to `medium` — the shipped fallback,
  which is separate from `defaultTier` though both resolve here to the same
  profile, which is also the rung the parent runs.

  The concrete provider/model references are intentionally in the user-owned
  chezmoi policy rather than in the public extension package. `max` is a real
  rung on gpt-5.6-luna. gpt-6-astra replaced gpt-5.6-sol at the top on
  2026-09-06 and then spread downward: private runs it at `medium` and `high`,
  work at `high`. That intermediate rung only exists because astra ships the
  full low|medium|high|xhigh|max map where sol had {minimal: low, xhigh: xhigh,
  max: max}. private `high` stays at `xhigh` because that is astra's own IQ
  peak, not because it is capped -- `max` scores lower (111.9) and `ultra` lower
  still (104.2), both slower and dearer. There is nothing above xhigh to buy.

  COST -- and the unit price is the wrong number to read it off. gpt-6-astra is
  dearer per token than gpt-5.6-sol ($10/$50/$1 per M input/output/cacheRead
  against $5/$30/$0.5, and $20/$75/$2 against $10/$45/$1 above Codex's 272K
  premium line), but it spends far fewer tokens reaching the same place, and the
  token count moves more than the price does. Measured per task (codexradar
  intelligence-efficiency, 2026-09-06):

    rung          $/task  tokens     IQ     where it runs
    astra/xhigh    4.12     1.93M  112.5    private high   (was sol/xhigh: 5.25)
    astra/high     2.84     1.32M  104.1    private medium + parent; work high
    luna/max       0.53    17.86M  100.9    private low; work medium + parent
    luna/xhigh     0.33    10.92M   87.5    work low

  So the same 2026-09-06 change made `high` both cheaper and better, while
  `medium` got ~5.4x dearer for +3.2 IQ. On the private machine it is now the
  DEFAULT rung -- shared by the parent session and by every spawn that names no
  tier -- that drives the bill, not the top one. That is the reverse of the
  2026-09-02 audit, where `high` was 93.5% of spend, and it is worth
  re-measuring after a couple of days of real use.
  Verify model availability with Pi's model registry before changing these pins.

  maxConcurrent is 40, and what that number governs is about to widen. Through
  pi-subagents 1.9.x it bounds direct Agent fan-out only: pi-workflows has no way
  to read it, so a workflow run applies a package-level cap of its own — the core
  count minus two, at most 16 — and a fan-out there stays narrow no matter what
  this says, leaving the rest of the pool idle.

  From pi-subagents 1.10.0 with pi-workflows 1.7.0 the two stop being separate
  numbers. A workflow requests this value by name over the ping handshake and
  uses it as both its default width and its ceiling, so this key becomes the one
  place that sizes every background agent on the machine — direct Agent fan-out
  and workflow fan-out alike — and 40 is what a workflow run will actually
  dispatch at. Raising it raises both; there is no longer a separate workflow cap
  to raise instead, which is the point: one setting, one fleet.

  Keep it at or below 64. pi-workflows clamps to 64 of its own as a runaway-script
  guard, so a larger value here would widen direct Agent fan-out while workflows
  silently stopped at 64 — two numbers diverging with nothing to say so. At 40 the
  clamp does not bite and this file is exact.

  This file branches on chezmoi data in exactly one place: the four `.work`
  ternaries below, which pick the per-machine rungs listed at the top. `high`'s
  MODEL is unconditional — astra on both machines — and only its effort branches.
  The tier NAMES never branch, which is what lets pi-workflows' strengths table in
  workflows/settings.json.tmpl map onto both machines unchanged.
*/ -}}
{{- $highModel := "gpt-6-astra" -}}
{{- $highThink := ternary "high" "xhigh" .work -}}
{{- $mediumModel := ternary "gpt-5.6-luna" "gpt-6-astra" .work -}}
{{- $mediumThink := ternary "max" "high" .work -}}
{{- $lowThink := ternary "xhigh" "max" .work -}}
{{- $model := "openai-codex/gpt-5.6-luna" -}}
{
  "maxConcurrent": 40,
  "defaultMaxTurns": 640,
  "defaultMaxToolCalls": 1024,
  "defaultMaxTokens": 3000000,
  "graceTurns": 8,
  "defaultJoinMode": "smart",
  "schedulingEnabled": true,
  "scopeModels": false,
  "disableDefaultAgents": true,
  "fallbackSubagent": "none",
  "toolDescriptionMode": "full",
  "fleetView": true,
  "outputTranscript": true,
  "agentTiers": {
    "defaultTier": "medium",
    "profiles": {
      "low": {
        "description": "Search, navigation, and read-heavy work where breadth beats depth",
        "model": "{{ $model }}",
        "thinking": "{{ $lowThink }}"
      },
      "medium": {
        "description": "Implementation, review, and verification",
        "model": "openai-codex/{{ $mediumModel }}",
        "thinking": "{{ $mediumThink }}"
      },
      "high": {
        "description": "Architecture and high-risk review, where a wrong call is expensive to reverse",
        "model": "openai-codex/{{ $highModel }}",
        "thinking": "{{ $highThink }}"
      }
    }
  }
}
